# GuessWatts - Data Model

`js/calculator.js` owns the formulas. This doc explains them in prose and names where each one
lives; where the two disagree, the code is right and this doc is the bug. `scripts/selftest.js`
imports the calculator and pins golden values, so a formula change shows up there first.

## Data Sources

Two CSV files from the ERSE simulator, `data/Precos_ELEGN.csv` (prices per offer, power and tariff
type) and `data/CondComerciais.csv` (commercial conditions, contacts, links).
`scripts/download-erse.js` fetches them and records ERSE's export date in `data/meta.json`;
`scripts/build-offers.js` joins them into `data/offers.json`; `scripts/validate-data.js` checks the
result. The refresh schedule lives in `.github/workflows/update-erse.yml`, and the page shows the
ERSE date from `meta.json`, so no other file states how often the data changes.

`data/tar.json` holds the network-access (TAR) fixed term per power, copied by hand from ERSE's
yearly tariff directive (its `source` and `sourceUrl` say which one). `validate-data.js --tar-year`
fails when its `year` is not the current one, and the workflow turns that into an issue.

## CSV Columns

### Precos_ELEGN.csv

The header is `COM;Pot_Cont;Escalao;ORD;COD_Proposta;Contagem;TF;TV|TVFV|TVP;TVV|TVC;TVVz;TFGN;TVGN`.
The build reads `COM` (supplier code), `COD_Proposta` (offer code), `Pot_Cont` (contracted power,
kVA), `Contagem` (1 simple, 2 bi-hourly, 3 tri-hourly), `TF` (fixed term, €/day without VAT) and the
three energy columns. The gas columns and `Escalao`/`ORD` are ignored. There is one row per offer,
power and tariff type; decimals use a comma and the build turns them into numbers.

The three energy columns are shared between tariff types, and `Contagem` decides what each one means:

| Column | Simple (1) | Bi-hourly (2) | Tri-hourly (3) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `TV\|TVFV\|TVP` | the single price | fora vazio | ponta |
| `TVV\|TVC` | unused | vazio | cheias |
| `TVVz` | unused | unused | vazio |

`offers.json` keeps these three keys exactly as ERSE names them. `calculateMonthlyCost` reads them
and falls back to the split names (`TV`, `TVV`, `TVFV`, `TVC`, `TVP`) only for hand-made test
offers; offers.json never has those.

### CondComerciais.csv

The build reads, per `COM` + `COD_Proposta`: `NomeProposta`, `Fornecimento`, `Segmento`,
`Data ini`, `Data fim`, `FiltroFidelização`, `DuracaoContrato`, `FiltroRestrições`,
`FiltroPrecosIndex_ELE` (or `FiltroPrecosIndex` in exports before 2026), `ConsIni_ELE`,
`ConsFim_ELE`, `CustoServicos_c/IVA (€/ano)`, `ContactoComercialTel`, `LinkOfertaCom`, `LinkCOM`,
and the free-text columns used for lock-in and promotion detection (`TxTFidelização`, `TxTOferta`,
`TxTRestricoesAdic` and others). `REQUIRED_COLUMNS` in `scripts/validate-data.js` is the list the
refresh refuses to run without.

## How offers.json Is Built

`buildOffers` in `scripts/build-offers.js` joins each price row to its condition on
`COM` + `COD_Proposta` and keeps a row only when:

- the condition exists and `Fornecimento` is `ELE` (gas and dual offers are dropped);
- `Segmento` is not `Ndom` (ADR-020; `Tod` offers are open to everyone and stay);
- `TF`, `TV|TVFV|TVP` and `Pot_Cont` are all above zero;
- it is the first row for its `COM`, `COD_Proposta`, `Pot_Cont` and `Contagem`.

Each offer carries the price fields above plus the metadata from `extractCampaignMetadata`:

- `tariffName`, `website` (`LinkOfertaCom`, else `LinkCOM`), `phone`, `fornecimento`, `segmento`.
- `validFrom` and `validTo` (`Data ini`, `Data fim`, DD/MM/YYYY, either may be empty).
- `consMinYear` and `consMaxYear` (the annual consumption band in kWh, or null) and
  `serviceCostYear` (mandatory services, €/year with VAT, 0 when none).
- `isIndexed` (indexed prices; the result card shows the price date) and `isRestricted`
  (a conditioned offer: members, referral codes, other contracts).
- `hasLockIn`, `lockInMonths` and `lockInSource` from `detectLockIn`: `FiltroFidelização` S or N
  decides, and the free text is read only when the flag is empty, ignoring negated phrases such as
  "sem fidelização".
- `promotion`: metadata only, never applied to any cost.
- `cycleType` from `detectCycleType`: `'daily'` or `'weekly'` when `NomeProposta` says so, otherwise
  null, which is the case for most ERSE offers.

Nothing date-dependent is baked in: whether an offer is active is decided in the browser on the day
of the visit. The build also writes `meta.json` `build`, including the supplier codes it saw
(`build.providers`).

## Constants

The tax and default constants live in `js/config.js`: `VAT`, `VAT_REDUCED`,
`REDUCED_VAT_MAX_POWER`, `REDUCED_VAT_KWH`, `REDUCED_VAT_KWH_LARGE_HOUSEHOLD`,
`REDUCED_FIXED_VAT_MAX_POWER`, `DGEG_FEE`, `AUDIOVISUAL_TAX`, `DAYS_PER_MONTH` and `DEFAULT_POWER`
(4.6 kVA, the power estimate mode assumes). The rest live in `js/calculator.js`:

- `IEC_KWH`: the special consumption tax per kWh.
- `BI_HORARIA`: the default bi-hourly split, 40% vazio and 60% fora vazio, which is the ERSE
  simulator's assumption (Manual do Simulador, "Pressupostos considerados", p. 15).
- `TRI_HORARIA`: the default tri-hourly split. ERSE publishes none, so it has no official source.
- `FALLBACK_TF` and `FALLBACK_TV`: the typical offer used only when no data is loaded.
- `MIN_ESTIMATE_KWH` and `MAX_ESTIMATE_KWH`: the clamp on the consumption estimate (50 to 5000 kWh).

## Monthly Cost

`calculateMonthlyCost(offer, consumption, power, distribution, context)` returns the cost of one
offer for 30 days (`DAYS_PER_MONTH`), with VAT. The fixed term is `TF` times the days. The energy
term multiplies the consumption by the offer's price, or, for bi- and tri-hourly tariffs, by each
period's price weighted by the split. The split is the one read from the person's PDF bill when
there is one (`js/pdf-service.js`), otherwise `BI_HORARIA` or `TRI_HORARIA`. The cycle (daily or
weekly) changes the split on a real bill, never the prices.

VAT is applied per component, as ERSE's "ERSExplica: Aplicação do IVA na fatura de eletricidade"
does:

- **Energy:** the reduced rate on the first `REDUCED_VAT_KWH` per 30 days
  (`REDUCED_VAT_KWH_LARGE_HOUSEHOLD` for households of 5 or more) at `REDUCED_VAT_MAX_POWER` or
  below, the standard rate on the rest (Lei 38/2024). For bi- and tri-hourly tariffs the reduced
  share is prorated across the periods, as in ERSE's Exemplo 3.
- **Fixed term:** the reduced rate on its TAR share (from `data/tar.json`) at
  `REDUCED_FIXED_VAT_MAX_POWER` or below, the standard rate on the rest (DL 60/2019).
- **IEC** (`IEC_KWH`) and the **DGEG fee** (`DGEG_FEE`, Portaria 311/2002): the standard rate.
- **Audiovisual contribution** (`AUDIOVISUAL_TAX`): the reduced rate.

Mandatory services are added as `serviceCostYear / 12`, as ERSE's simulator does.

The `context` carries `tar` (the loaded `data/tar.json`), `largeHousehold` (the household checkbox),
`reducedVatKwh` (an override the selftest uses) and `asOf` (the day for the eligibility checks).
The selftest reproduces ERSE's examples 1, 3 and 4 to the cent; they use the 2022 thresholds (100
and 150 kWh), passed as `reducedVatKwh`. Not modelled: the social tariff, and Madeira and Azores
rates.

`calculateAnnualEffectiveCost` is the monthly cost times 12. `calculateMonthlyCostWithPromotion`
returns the base cost unchanged: promotions are never applied.

## Estimating Consumption From a Bill

In estimate mode the person gives only the bill in euros. `estimateConsumption` inverts
`calculateMonthlyCost` by bisection on `typicalOffer`: a simple-tariff offer at `DEFAULT_POWER` whose
fixed term is the median `TF` and whose energy price is the median simple-tariff price among the
offers at that power that are active that day. The estimate therefore uses the same formula and
taxes as the costs shown afterwards. The result is rounded and clamped to `MIN_ESTIMATE_KWH` to
`MAX_ESTIMATE_KWH`. With no data loaded the typical offer uses `FALLBACK_TF` and `FALLBACK_TV`. The
selftest pins the round trip (kWh to bill to kWh, within 1 kWh).

## Eligibility

`isOfferActive` drops an offer outside `validFrom`/`validTo` on the day of the visit.
`isOfferEligible` adds the consumption band: the monthly consumption times 12 must sit inside
`consMinYear`/`consMaxYear` when they are set.

## Ranking

`compare(offers, input, context)` is the one entry point every flow uses (estimate form, manual
form, PDF). In estimate mode it estimates the consumption, ranks simple-tariff offers at
`DEFAULT_POWER` with `findBestOffer`, and returns `marketMedianCost` beside the best offer, with no
personal saving. In precise mode it ranks with `findBestOfferForTariff` for the person's power,
tariff type and split, and, when the person named a supplier, adds `currentSupplierSaving`.

Both find functions keep an offer only when it matches the power (to 0.01 kVA in
`findBestOfferForTariff`) and the tariff type, has `TF` and `TV|TVFV|TVP` above zero, has no lock-in
(`hasLockIn`), is not conditioned (`isRestricted`, ADR-022) and passes `isOfferEligible`. Offers whose
cost comes out zero or not a number are dropped too.

The order is the annual cost (`calculateAnnualEffectiveCost`), then the monthly base cost, then the
tariff name (falling back to `COD_Proposta`, then `COM`), case-insensitive, so the output is stable.
Both functions throw when no offer qualifies.

`marketMedianCost` is the median monthly cost of the offers the same filters keep.
`currentSupplierSaving` compares the best offer with the cheapest offer of the person's current
supplier for the same power and tariff type, lock-in and conditioned offers included, since the
person may be on any of them.

## Supplier Names

ERSE publishes supplier codes (`COM`) only, never names. `PROVIDERS` in `js/config.js` names every
code in `offers.json`, and the dropdown and the result card read it. The dropdown follows the codes
in `meta.json` `build.providers` (`matchProvidersToData` in `js/ui-components.js`): a named supplier
with no offers this week drops out, a new code shows under its ERSE code, and "Outro" is always
last. Supplier churn never turns the refresh red: the selftest only warns, and
`validate-data.js --supplier-names` fails, and the workflow opens an issue, until the code gets a
name in `PROVIDERS`.

## Data Bounds

The sanity bounds on the data (offer and supplier counts, price ranges, the jump against HEAD, the
TAR values) are `BOUNDS` in `scripts/validate-data.js`; that file owns them.
